menu "Embedded Departure Board"
source "Kconfig.zephyr"

#### LED DISPLAY TEST SETTINGS ####
config LED_DISPLAY_TEST
  bool "Select to run LED display test instead of main application"

#### STOP SETTINGS ####
config STOP_ID
  string "Bus stop ID"
  help
    Boot seed for the runtime stop ID (src/stop_id.h) and nothing more:
    update_stop's boot gate parks every fetch and display write until
    the shadow's first target_config supplies the real stop, so this
    value can no longer reach the network or the displays on a managed
    sign. It survives for builds that run without a shadow.

config STOP_MAX_ROUTES
  int "Maximum number of routes expected at the configured stop"
  default 5

config ROUTE_MAX_DEPARTURES
  int "Maximum number of departures expected for EACH route"
  default 2

#### SIGN SETTINGS ####

config UPDATE_STOP_FREQUENCY_SECONDS
  int "The frequency at which stop departure times are updated in seconds"
  default 30
  help
    Boot-time default only: runtime-overridable from the pigeon shadow's
    "update_stop_interval" key (net/pigeon_client.c), clamped there to
    5-45s -- the ceiling keeps main's watchdog-feeding loop inside
    MAX_TIME_INACTIVE_BEFORE_RESET_MS.

    The compiled default must stay armed from boot even though passes
    before the first shadow sync are skipped dark: those skipped passes
    are where main feeds the watchdog, so a sign waiting out a long
    platform outage stays alive to sync at all.

config MAX_TIME_INACTIVE_BEFORE_RESET_MS
  int "Maximum amount of time the system can remain inactive before it resets in milliseconds"
  default 60000

config UPDATE_FAILURES_BEFORE_RESET
  int "Consecutive failed update cycles that trigger a reset"
  default 3
  help
    Reset once this many update cycles have failed back to back. 1
    restores the old behaviour of resetting on any single failure.

    A reset costs a full LTE reattach, so it is only worth doing for a
    fault that persists -- a lone upstream hiccup resolves long before
    the reboot finishes, and rebooting for one has the sign dark for
    longer than the failure itself would have. Below the threshold the
    display simply holds its last state, and the loop keeps feeding the
    watchdog, so a genuinely wedged device is still caught.

config DNS_FAILURES_BEFORE_MODEM_RESTART
  int "Consecutive resolver failures that trigger a modem restart"
  default 60
  help
    Restart the modem -- offline functional mode, then normal -- once
    this many Swiftly fetches in a row have failed inside
    getaddrinfo(). A resolved lookup clears the count. 0 disables the
    recovery.

    This exists for the modem DNS wedge in this board's history:
    getaddrinfo() started returning EAGAIN and kept returning it for
    14+ hours, and nothing short of restarting the modem cleared it.
    The firmware never attempted one.

    The default is deliberately generous. 60 failures is half an hour
    at the 30s default cadence, and the wedge is a many-hour
    phenomenon, so nothing transient should ever spend an LTE reattach
    on this. Note that the threshold counts failures rather than
    seconds: the fetch cadence is shadow-tunable down to 5s, which
    would shrink the same count to five minutes.

    Where this can fire is narrower than it looks, and worth
    understanding before retuning it. A failed resolve fails the whole
    update cycle, and UPDATE_FAILURES_BEFORE_RESET already reboots the
    sign after three of those in a row -- a reboot that powers the
    modem off and brings it back up, which is a superset of what this
    does and arrives twenty times sooner. So in ordinary operation the
    streak cannot exceed three and this never runs.

    The window it is really for is a FOTA download, where that reset
    policy is deliberately suppressed for as long as the transfer runs,
    up to its 60-minute ceiling -- and a sustained transfer degrading
    the link until DNS failed against every host is something this
    hardware has actually done, during the first live OTA. In that
    window this is the only self-recovery the device has, which is also
    why the threshold sits well inside the ceiling rather than past it.

    Only the Swiftly fetch feeds the count. The pigeon client resolves
    against a different host on its own thread and schedule, so mixing
    it in would leave a streak meaning nothing in particular.

#### LIGHT_SENSOR SETTINGS ####

config LIGHT_SENSOR
  bool "Enable I2C ambient light sensor"
  default y

#### HTTP stop request settings ####

config HTTP_REQUEST_RETRY_COUNT
  int "Defines the number of HTTP request retries on failure (i.e. 404)"
  default 0
  help
    Boot-time default only: runtime-overridable from the pigeon shadow's
    "http_retry_count" key (src/runtime_config.h), clamped to 0-3.
    Inert until the first shadow sync: its only consumer is the Swiftly
    fetch, which update_stop's boot gate holds until then.

config HTTP_REQUEST_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS
  int "Delay before the first HTTP request retry, in milliseconds"
  default 1000
  help
    Wait between a failed request and the next attempt, doubling per retry
    up to HTTP_REQUEST_RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS. Zero restores the old
    retry-immediately behaviour.

    This exists because the upstream failures observed in production
    arrive in clusters rather than as isolated events, so an instant retry
    reissues the request inside the same upstream disturbance that just
    rejected it and fails for the same reason. Spacing the attempts is
    what makes the retry budget worth spending; raising the count alone
    only buys more attempts inside the same bad moment.

config HTTP_REQUEST_RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS
  int "Ceiling on the per-retry backoff delay, in milliseconds"
  default 4000
  help
    Caps the doubling. The retry loop feeds the watchdog at the top of
    every attempt, so the interval it must stay inside is one attempt plus
    one backoff, not the sum of all of them -- but the cap keeps that
    single interval far below MAX_TIME_INACTIVE_BEFORE_RESET_MS even if an
    attempt itself runs long.

config STOP_JSON_BUF_SIZE
  int "Defines the max size for stop JSON response body receive buffer"
  default 4096

## SWIFTLY ##
config SWIFTLY_API_HOSTNAME
  string "Swiftly API hostname used to retrieve stop predictions data"
  default "api.goswift.ly"

config SWIFTLY_API_PATH
  string "Swiftly API HTTP path used to retrieve stop predictions data"
  default "/real-time/pioneer-valley-pvta/predictions"

config SWIFTLY_API_NUMBER_OF_PREDICTIONS
  string "The number of future predictions to return per route/stop in the response"
  default "1"

#### PIGEON (PidgeIoT device management) SETTINGS ####
# CONFIG_PIGEON/_ENDPOINT/_TOKEN/etc. are the `pigeon` module's own Kconfig
# (west-managed, see app/west.yml) -- this app-level namespace is only for
# knobs specific to how THIS app drives the library (see net/pigeon_client.c).

config PIGEON_CLIENT_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS
  int "Default shadow/telemetry poll interval in seconds"
  default 300
  help
    Fallback used until the shadow's target_config supplies its own
    "telemetry_interval" (see net/pigeon_client.c) -- same convention as
    pigeon-examples' https_init sample. Kept coarse by default: this is a
    mains-powered sign, but the LTE data budget for the poll's shadow GET
    plus telemetry POSTs still isn't free.

    The one knob that cannot wait for the shadow: it paces the very sync
    that delivers the shadow's own value, and the telemetry it schedules
    is the fleet's first diagnostic signal on every boot.

config PIGEON_CLIENT_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
  int "Pigeon client thread stack size (bytes)"
  default 5632
  help
    Stack for the dedicated thread all PidgeIoT I/O runs on (see
    net/pigeon_client.c) -- sized for the pigeon library's HTTPS client
    plus JSON shadow decode, NOT for a TLS handshake: pigeon's sockets
    are plain offloaded IPPROTO_TLS_1_2, so the handshake runs inside
    the modem, never on this stack. Evidence (2026-08-08 RAM diet):
    thread-analyzer peak 3112 on a full shadow+telemetry cycle; the
    FOTA path (dfu_target/flash_img frames on top) ran two complete
    real downloads within the earlier 6144 -- 5632 keeps >=1KB over a
    conservative FOTA estimate. Bump back first if a FOTA soak ever
    shows an MPU stack fault on this thread.

endmenu
